{"title":"Fenland Biogas","sub":"A digester that runs on other people's waste","type":"Investment / capacity","industry":"Energy","geo":"United Kingdom","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"NPV · sensitivity · revenue risk","prompt":"A farming co-operative in eastern England wants to build a $15M anaerobic digester, which turns food waste and manure into biogas for electricity. It would earn money from three sources: selling electricity, charging food companies to take their waste (gate fees), and selling the leftover material as fertiliser. The co-op's chair asks whether it's a sound investment.","clarify":[{"q":"Yearly income?","a":"Electricity about $1.6M; gate fees about $1.2M; fertiliser about $0.2M."},{"q":"Running costs?","a":"About $1.3M a year for staff, maintenance and transport."},{"q":"Life and discount rate?","a":"20 years; the co-op's lender uses 8%."},{"q":"Gate fees?","a":"Three new digesters are planned within 30 miles. Gate fees elsewhere fell by about half when new digesters opened nearby. No long-term waste contracts are signed yet."}],"structure":{"root":"Should the co-op build the digester?","branches":[{"head":"Income","items":["Electricity","Gate fees","Fertiliser"]},{"head":"Costs","items":["Build","Running"]},{"head":"NPV and risk","items":["Base case","If gate fees fall"]},{"head":"Protection","items":["Long-term waste contracts"]}]},"hypothesis":"Gate fees are a large share of income and could fall as competitors open. I'd test the NPV if they halve.","questions":[{"q":"What is the NPV in the base case?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Yearly cash flow: $1.6M + $1.2M + $0.2M − $1.3M = <b>$1.7M</b>. Discount factor (20 years, 8%): 9.82.</p> <p>NPV = −$15M + $1.7M × 9.82 = <b>+$1.7M</b>.</p>"},{"q":"What if gate fees halve as new digesters open?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"393.86pt\" height=\"160.89904pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 393.86 160.89904\" xmlns=\"http://www